As another user has suggested, make sure the attributes of the file are
such that the game can delete it.  But if you haven't monkeyed around
with it then these attributes shouldn't be changed.

If other levels are making bones, and they are being deleted normally
upon being loaded, then the problem must be with this file, and
deleting manually would be called for to fix it.  (It is okay to delete
bones files, in my experience, it mess up your copy of the game.)

If all levels are creating, but not deleting bones, then it might have
to do with the permissions of the directory or the game, perhaps?  If
it's a game bug it would be a weird one, perhaps having to do with a
file with a malformed filesystem entry?  Maybe running a consistency
check (chkdsk or Scandisk) on the disk would help?



